      "beats": [
        {
          "say": "An object reference is a handle to a result, with ownership and lifetime rules. The process that submits a task generally owns its result reference. A different worker computes the bytes. Keeping the owner and the physical storage separate makes several failure cases easier to understand.\n",
          "start": 0.4,
          "end": 17.752,
          "duration": 17.352,
          "until": 18.352
        },
        {
          "say": "A consumer on another node needs access to the value. Our constructed trace adds a second stored copy while preserving the original owner. Eligible arrays can use shared-memory views locally. Remote transfer, retained storage and reference bookkeeping still consume resources even when the driver avoids materializing the payload.\n",
          "start": 18.352,
          "end": 38.68,
          "duration": 20.328,
          "until": 39.28
        },
        {
          "say": "Losing one copy can be repaired from another. Reconstructing all lost copies of a task result requires live ownership information, usable lineage and available dependencies. A value placed directly into storage has no generating task to replay. Surviving bytes alone also cannot restore a lost owner's metadata contract.\n",
          "start": 39.28,
          "end": 60.304,
          "duration": 21.024,
          "until": 60.904
        }
      ],
